  "response": "## Merge verdict: block\n\nThe scan reports 55 total findings, but the requested five categories contain 36 findings. I would block this empty-baseline PR on two oversized public/core modules and eight assertion-free behavioral tests. The remainder should be warnings or ignores; `agent_risk` is prioritization evidence, not a merge threshold by itself.\n\n### Block merge\n\n| Finding | Severity / agent risk | Decision rationale |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `crime_00003` — `lib/command.js`, 2,179 lines | high / **0.30** | Block. This is the CLI’s central implementation and is 7.3× the file threshold. Landing the entire codebase this way establishes a severe context and coupling hazard. Require a responsibility-based split or a documented near-term decomposition with clear module boundaries. |\n| `crime_00004` — `typings/index.d.ts`, 891 lines | high / **0.30** | Block. It is 3× the threshold, has `test_gap: 0.75`, and defines a public type surface. Large declaration edits are especially prone to incomplete cross-interface updates. |\n| `crime_00012`–`crime_00019` — assertion-free `command.exitOverride` tests | medium / **0.27 each** | Block as one cluster. Eight tests claim important error and exit behavior but contain zero detected assertions: custom errors, help/version exits, subcommand completion, mandatory options, and invalid choices. These are behavioral protections, so the PR should demonstrate observable outcomes before merging. |\n\nA block does not mean every finding must be eliminated. It means these three remediation units—the two large core/public files and the assertion-free behavioral-test cluster—need resolution or explicit maintainer acceptance.\n\n### Warn\n\n| Finding(s) | Severity / agent risk | Decision rationale |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `crime_00001` — `\"PORT\"` across five files | medium / **0.46** | Warn, highest-scored requested finding. The high score is driven partly by `test_gap: 0.75`, but most occurrences are examples and a type-test