# Ignore\n\n| Finding(s) | Severity / agent risk | Decision rationale |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `crime_00020`–`crime_00027` — assertion-free `ts-imports` tests | medium / **0.27 each** | Ignore, assuming these are compile-time import/type checks. Successful TypeScript compilation is the assertion, so runtime `expect` calls would add little. Confirm the files are actually included in the typecheck; otherwise move them to “block.” |\n| `crime_00053` — `CHANGELOG.md`, 1,243 lines | low / **0.12** | Ignore. Changelogs naturally grow append-only and are normally searched by release/version. Splitting introduces navigation and release-process overhead without materially reducing change risk. |\n\n### Complete requested enumeration\n\n- Dead/commented code: `crime_00008`\n- Prose rule in comments: `crime_00028`\n- Magic literal scatter: `crime_00001`\n- Weak-test signal: `crime_00012`–`crime_00027`, `crime_00034`–`crime_00042`\n- Large files: `crime_00003`, `crime_00004`, `crime_00006`, `crime_00043`, `crime_00045`, `crime_00053`–`crime_00055`\n\nThis accounts for all **36** findings in the requested categories. The other 19 findings—large functions, naming drift, duplication, synchronous I/O, documentation drift, and hardcoded localhost—are outside this review’s stated scope.",
